<!-- ===== SECTION 1: THE FEATURE STORE PROBLEM ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="feature-store-problem">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>The Feature Store Problem: Why Manual Engineering Doesn't Scale</h2>

  <p>Imagine a bank with five different ML models: fraud detection, customer churn prediction, credit risk assessment, loan recommendation, and customer lifetime value. Each model needs features like transaction velocity, account age, average transaction amount, and customer risk score. Without a feature store, each model team implements these features independently in Python, SQL, or Spark.</p>

  <p>This duplication creates multiple problems. First, different implementations might compute the same feature differently: one team uses a 30-day window, another uses 60 days. Both claim to compute "transaction velocity," but they're not compatible. Second, when feature definitions need to change (regulatory requirement, bug fix), you must update code in five places, risking inconsistency. Third, at serving time, your API must recompute features, but the computation logic differs between training and serving, leading to training-serving skew.</p>

  <!-- ANALOGY BOX -->
  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Imagine your software company had no shared logging library. Every application implemented logging independently: different formats, different levels, different backends. When you need to change logging (compliance requirement, performance issue), you update twenty applications, each with slightly different code. Feature stores are like a shared feature engineering library: define features once, use everywhere, change in one place.</p>
  </div>

