<!-- ===== SECTION 2: ARCHITECTURE ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="feature-store-architecture">
  <span class="section-label">Architecture</span>
  <h2>Feature Store Architecture: Batch and Real-Time Serving</h2>

  <p>Modern feature stores have two distinct paths: batch serving and real-time serving. Batch features are computed on a schedule (daily, hourly) and stored in a data warehouse for training and batch predictions. Real-time features are cached in low-latency databases (Redis, DynamoDB) for online inference where millisecond response times are critical.</p>

  <p>The feature store sits between raw data and ML models. Data engineers define feature computation logic once. The feature store orchestrates batch computation jobs and manages real-time caches. Models simply request features without caring about underlying complexity.</p>

  <h3>Batch Features for Training</h3>

  <p>Computed daily or hourly and stored in data warehouse tables. Used when building training datasets and for batch predictions. Example: customer lifetime value, average purchase amount, churn risk score. Latency requirements: seconds to minutes are acceptable.</p>

  <h3>Real-Time Features for Online Inference</h3>

  <p>Computed on-demand or pre-computed and cached. Must be retrieved in milliseconds. Example: current user session duration, recent transaction count, current account balance. Latency requirements: milliseconds are mandatory.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Using Feast feature store (popular open-source)</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">feast</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="bi">FeatureStore</span>

<span class="cm"># Initialize feature store</span>
<span class="va">fs</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">FeatureStore</span>(<span class="va">repo_path</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"./feature_repo"</span>)

<span class="cm"># TRAINING: Get historical features for dataset creation</span>
<span class="va">training_df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">fs</span>.<span class="fn">get_historical_features</span>(
    <span class="va">entity_df</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">customer_ids</span>,  <span class="cm"># Your target entities</span>
    <span class="va">features</span><span class="op">=</span>[
        <span class="st">"customer:age"</span>,
        <span class="st">"customer:total_spend"</span>,
        <span class="st">"transactions:avg_amount_30d"</span>,
        <span class="st">"transactions:velocity_7d"</span>
    ]
).<span class="fn">to_df</span>()

<span class="cm"># SERVING: Get features for online prediction (same code!)</span>
<span class="va">online_features</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">fs</span>.<span class="fn">get_online_features</span>(
    <span class="va">entity_rows</span><span class="op">=</span>[{<span class="st">"customer_id"</span>: <span class="nm">12345</span>}],
    <span class="va">features</span><span class="op">=</span>[
        <span class="st">"customer:age"</span>,
        <span class="st">"customer:total_spend"</span>,
        <span class="st">"transactions:avg_amount_30d"</span>,
        <span class="st">"transactions:velocity_7d"</span>
    ]
)

<span class="cm"># Notice: Same feature list, identical API, no skew!</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We define features once (customer age, spending, transaction patterns). During training, we request historical features for a dataset. During serving, we request the same features for a single customer. The feature store handles the complexity: batch computation for training, caching for serving. The model code is identical in both cases.</p>
  </div>

