<!-- ===== SECTION 3: IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="feature-store-implementation">
  <span class="section-label">Practical Implementation</span>
  <h2>Implementing a Feature Store: From Simple to Enterprise</h2>

  <p>Starting a feature store doesn't require enterprise software. You can begin with a simple approach: define features in YAML, compute them on a schedule with Airflow or Cron, expose via REST API, cache hot features in Redis. Evolve to frameworks like Feast as your organization scales.</p>

  <h3>Phase 1: Simple Warehouse + API (Months 1-3)</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Define features in YAML or simple config file</li>
    <li>Compute features nightly with SQL/Spark jobs</li>
    <li>Store in data warehouse (Snowflake, Redshift, BigQuery)</li>
    <li>Expose via lightweight REST API</li>
    <li>Focus on 10-20 most valuable features first</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Phase 2: Open-Source Feature Store (Months 4-9)</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Implement Feast or similar framework</li>
    <li>Establish feature definitions and lineage tracking</li>
    <li>Set up batch and real-time serving paths</li>
    <li>Enable feature discovery and documentation</li>
    <li>Scale to 100+ features across multiple teams</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Phase 3: Enterprise Solution (Months 10+)</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Deploy managed feature store (Tecton, SageMaker Feature Store)</li>
    <li>Implement sophisticated caching and optimization</li>
    <li>Enable A/B testing and feature experiments</li>
    <li>Advanced governance and compliance features</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Feature definition in Feast (simple and clear)</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">feast</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="bi">FeatureView</span>, <span class="bi">Entity</span>, <span class="bi">FileSource</span>

<span class="cm"># Define entities (the business objects you compute features for)</span>
<span class="va">customer</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">Entity</span>(
    <span class="va">name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"customer"</span>,
    <span class="va">description</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"A customer in our business"</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Define feature views (sets of related features)</span>
<span class="va">customer_features</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">FeatureView</span>(
    <span class="va">name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"customer_features"</span>,
    <span class="va">entities</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="va">customer</span>],
    <span class="va">ttl</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">86400</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">7</span>,  <span class="cm"># 7 days cache</span>
    <span class="va">batch_source</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="fn">FileSource</span>(
        <span class="va">path</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"s3://features/customers.parquet"</span>
    ),
    <span class="va">features</span><span class="op">=</span>[
        <span class="st">"customer:age"</span>,
        <span class="st">"customer:total_spend"</span>,
        <span class="st">"customer:account_age_days"</span>
    ]
)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We define entities (customers) and features associated with them. The FeatureView specifies where data comes from, cache TTL (time-to-live), and which features are included. Feast uses this definition to orchestrate batch computation and real-time caching automatically.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- INFOGRAPHIC 3: Implementation Timeline -->
