<!-- ===== SECTION 4: GOVERNANCE AND LINEAGE ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="feature-governance">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Concepts</span>
  <h2>Feature Governance and Lineage Tracking</h2>

  <p>As your feature store grows to hundreds or thousands of features, governance becomes critical. You need to know: which features are used by which models, which data sources feed each feature, who owns each feature, what transformations apply to it, and whether it's compliant with regulations.</p>

  <p>Feature lineage is a directed graph: raw data sources → transformations → computed features → models. When a regulatory requirement changes or a bug is discovered, you can trace through this graph to understand impact. If a feature definition changes, you know which models must be retrained.</p>

  <h3>Feature Metadata and Ownership</h3>

  <p>Every feature should have clear ownership, documentation, and governance metadata. This seems simple but is critical for scale. When a feature breaks, you need to know who to contact. When a new model needs similar features, you need discovery mechanisms so teams don't reinvent.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Feature metadata in Feast</span>
<span class="va">customer_features</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">FeatureView</span>(
    <span class="va">name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"customer_features"</span>,
    <span class="va">description</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"Core customer demographic and behavioral features"</span>,
    <span class="va">owner</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"data-eng-team@company.com"</span>,
    <span class="va">tags</span><span class="op">=</span>{
        <span class="st">"team"</span>: <span class="st">"data-eng"</span>,
        <span class="st">"domain"</span>: <span class="st">"customer"</span>,
        <span class="st">"gdpr_compliant"</span>: <span class="st">"yes"</span>
    },
    <span class="va">entities</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="va">customer</span>],
    <span class="va">features</span><span class="op">=</span>[
        <span class="fn">Feature</span>(
            <span class="va">name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"age"</span>,
            <span class="va">dtype</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">ValueType.INT32</span>,
            <span class="va">description</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"Customer age in years"</span>
        ),
        <span class="fn">Feature</span>(
            <span class="va">name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"total_spend"</span>,
            <span class="va">dtype</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">ValueType.FLOAT</span>,
            <span class="va">description</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"Total amount spent across all purchases"</span>
        )
    ]
)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We define not just the feature names but ownership, descriptions, tags, and GDPR compliance status. This metadata is critical for governance. When a compliance audit happens, you can query all GDPR-compliant features. When a feature breaks, you contact the owner immediately.</p>
  </div>

