<section class="section reveal" id="exercises">
  <span class="section-label">Practice</span>
  <h2>Hands-On Exercises</h2>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 1: Design Feature Definitions</h4>
    <p>For a customer churn prediction problem, design 10 features (5 customer-level, 5 transaction-level). For each: write a clear definition, specify computation logic (SQL or pseudocode), identify data sources, estimate batch freshness (hourly/daily/weekly), and specify real-time latency requirements. Document everything.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 2: Set Up Feast Locally</h4>
    <p>Initialize a Feast feature store with sample data. Define feature views for batch and real-time features. Register a feature view in your local Feast store. Retrieve features for training using <code>get_historical_features()</code> and for serving using <code>get_online_features()</code>. Verify that identical code produces features in both cases.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 3: Prevent Training-Serving Skew</h4>
    <p>Implement a feature ("customer velocity") that can be computed two ways: (1) batch SQL query for training, (2) Python on-demand for serving. Implement both, run them on sample data, verify they produce identical results. Document how your feature store prevents skew for this feature.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 4: Feature Lineage and Discovery</h4>
    <p>For a feature store with at least 10 features, document the lineage: raw data sources → SQL/Python transformations → computed features → which models use them. Create a simple lineage graph showing dependencies. Demonstrate impact analysis: if the source data changes, which features and models are affected?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 5: Multi-Team Feature Governance</h4>
    <p>Design a feature governance structure for a 20-person ML team across 3 sub-teams (fraud, churn, recommendation). Define: (1) feature ownership model, (2) request/approval workflow for new features, (3) SLA for freshness and availability, (4) documentation requirements, (5) GDPR/compliance audit mechanism.</p>
  </div>
